# netsurf: node reads/writes on a parsed frame's handles (patch 307)
# Generated 2026-10-19
# Base: seleron/vitabrowser (patch stack through 306)
#
# Item 7, stage 3 (design: docs/phase-0/19) -- the design's staged
# surface is complete with this patch. Patch 304's node handles
# exposed identity only; 306 added the document handle's queries.
# Stage 3 gives the node handles their reads and writes:
# textContent and innerHTML (get/set), getAttribute /
# setAttribute, parentNode / firstChild / childNodes traversal
# as handles, and appendChild / removeChild taking handles of
# the same child document.
#
#   - Every function is the realm's own entry point
#     (qjs_el_*_data) re-pointed at the handle's node, with patch
#     304's validation in front of it: qjs_fnode_valid first, so
#     an inert handle's reads answer undefined and its writes are
#     dropped, and a libdom exception (a hierarchy error, a node
#     that is not a child) drops the call instead of throwing
#     across the boundary. Traversal mints handles by the same
#     wrap as the roots and the query results; the parent of the
#     root element is the document node itself, wrapped as a
#     handle (nodeType 9), as a browser's parentNode answers
#     the Document.
#   - appendChild / removeChild accept only a live handle of
#     the SAME child document (captured content and owner
#     document equal; generation agreement is implied, both
#     sides having matched the current generation). A node of
#     the parent's own document is not a handle and is dropped
#     -- adopting across documents is outside the design, as is
#     parentDocument.appendChild(childHandle), which stays the
#     type error it always was.
#   - Writes perform the same libdom mutations the realm's
#     entry points perform (the textContent empty-string rule,
#     fixes1031; the fragment-parser innerHTML with its
#     head-then-body descent, fixes846/1007, parsed against the
#     CHILD's document; fixes926's attribute-kind classification
#     and 186/197's old-class mark) and then take the same dirty
#     mark through the CHILD realm's context, so the mark lands
#     on the child's content and the write flows through the
#     child's own mutation/reconvert machinery. The parent gets
#     no private invalidation path (doc 19).
#   - Recorded remainder: the realm's entry points also queue a
#     MutationObserver record by calling the observing realm's
#     polyfill queue (__msQueueMutation). Delivering that record
#     for a parent-made write would mean running child-realm JS
#     from the parent's turn -- the nested re-entry doc 19's
#     first constraint forbids -- so child-realm observers do
#     not receive records for parent-made writes. The writes
#     themselves are fully visible to the child's own reads,
#     which is what the gate proves. A C-side queued delivery is
#     a follow-up design question, not part of the staged
#     surface.
#
# Gate: testpages/pages/JS-frame-document.html extended in
# place, XDOC2 8/8 -> 10/10 (the gate script's expectation
# updated with it). Check 9 writes through handles -- traversal
# first, then textContent and an attribute on the child's #kid,
# innerHTML on its #box, a removeChild and an appendChild move
# -- and passes only when the child's own script reports the
# written state from its own reads: the write crossed. Check 10
# is the discipline: a parent-document node passed to
# appendChild is dropped, and after the frame navigates, the
# inert handle's writes are dropped without throwing and its
# stage 3 reads answer undefined. The page scored 5/10 before
# this patch (check 9 missing its surface, the flow stalling
# behind it). In Firefox/Camoufox checks 1-3, 6, 7 and 9 pass --
# the read/write surface behaves as a browser's does; 4, 5, 8
# and 10 are doc 19's handle semantics. JS-frame-xdoc.html
# stays XDOC 4/4 and JS-xorigin-frame.html stays XFRAME 9/9;
# the cross-origin null is untouched. With this patch, item
# 7's designed surface (doc 19, stages 1-3) is complete.
# Simulator only; the vita cross-build was not run (no
# vitaSDK on this VM). Device expectation is in Doc 10.
